.TH "LDM2" "1" "09/18/2026" "" ""
.SH "NAME"
ldm \- a display manager for LTSP
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fIldm\fR <vt[1\-N]> <:[0\-N]>
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
ldm(1) starts an X server and presents the user with a login screen, similar to the gdm(1) login prompt. instead of using the XDMCP protocol, ldm(1) uses ssh(1) connect to remote servers, then starts an Xsession either via ssh(1) X forwarding, or direct TCP/IP connection.

It was designed for the LTSP project, but could be used in a non\-ltsp environment as well.
.SH "OPTIONS"
<vt[1\-N]>
    tty where ldm will run

<:[0\-N]>
    X display that ldm uses.
.SH "CONFIGURATION"
ldm reads its settings from the environment. On an LTSP client that environment
comes from \fBlts.conf\fP(5), which is where every \fBLDM_\fR variable is
described; the ones that decide what the login screen says and how it looks are
summarised here.
.SS "What it says"
.IP "\fBLDM_USERNAME_PROMPT\fR, \fBLDM_PASSWORD_PROMPT\fR" 4
The two prompts beside the entry field. Each is Pango markup and reaches the
screen unchanged, so the value carries its own emphasis \(em the defaults are
\fB<b>Username</b>\fR and \fB<b>Password</b>\fR, translated through gettext.
.IP "\fBLDM_VERIFY_MESSAGE\fR" 4
The line shown while the password is being checked. Also Pango markup; the
default is a bold, translated "Verifying password.  Please wait.".
.IP "" 4
Setting any of these replaces the translation for that string. Leave them unset
and a localised image keeps its own wording.
.SS "How it looks"
.IP "\fBLDM_THEME\fR" 4
A directory under \fI/usr/share/ldm/themes\fR, or an absolute path. Files are
looked up in it one at a time and each one that is missing is taken from the
\fBltsp\fR theme instead \(em so a theme can consist of nothing but a
background image. The other side of that rule: a theme that does carry a
\fIgreeter.css\fR replaces the default one outright rather than adding to it.
.IP "\fBLDM_CLOCK_FORMAT\fR" 4
An \fBstrftime\fP(3) format, Pango markup allowed. Default
\fB"%Y\-%m\-%d, <b>%H:%M</b>"\fR.
.PP
Colours, fonts and spacing come from \fIgreeter.css\fR in the theme directory,
loaded as GTK3 CSS at \fBGTK_STYLE_PROVIDER_PRIORITY_APPLICATION\fR, which puts
it above the GTK theme. Five widgets carry a name so they can be addressed
separately:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
#ldm\-prompt        the Username / Password line beside the entry
#ldm\-status        the status line under it
#ldm\-host          host name and address
#ldm\-clock         the clock
#ldm\-preferences   the Preferences button
.fi
.RE
.PP
The Preferences button is in that list because it is drawn with relief
\fBNONE\fR: it has no ground of its own and sits straight on the background
image, exactly as the four labels do. A theme that lightens the labels and
forgets it leaves one word in the default near\-black.
.PP
A theme whose background is a dark photograph usually needs no more than the
following, and the shadow does more work than the colour \(em a photograph is
light in some places and dark in others:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
#ldm\-prompt, #ldm\-status, #ldm\-host, #ldm\-clock, #ldm\-preferences {
    color: #f2f2f2;
    text\-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.85);
}
.fi
.RE
.SH "FILES"
.IP "\fI/usr/share/ldm/themes/\fR" 4
The themes. \fBltsp\fR is the default and the fallback for every file another
theme does not carry.
.IP "\fI/var/log/ldm.log\fR" 4
Unless \fBLDM_SYSLOG\fR sends it elsewhere. On a thin client this lives in RAM
and dies with the session.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBlts.conf\fP(5), \fBstrftime\fP(3), \fBssh\fP(1)
